course: dictionary
lesson: 8328
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words and sentences around them, tells you which meaning is right. «The Chairman called Toad the incorrigible rogue and hardened ruffian.» Incorrigible means cannot be made better, so a rogue here is a dishonest, bad person.
explain: «You little rogue!» said Grandad with a smile. The smile and the word little are the clues. Rogue here is playful: it means a naughty but likeable person, often a child. Same word, very different feeling.
explain: In «a rogue wave», rogue is an adjective. It describes something that behaves differently from the rest and causes trouble or danger. Check by swapping in your meaning: a sudden, dangerous wave fits the sentence, so your meaning is right.
fact: «Rogue» can be a noun (a rogue, rogues) or an adjective (rogue).
fact: As a noun, «rogue» can mean a dishonest person who cheats or tricks people.
fact: Said playfully, «rogue» means a naughty but likeable person, often a child.
fact: As an adjective, «rogue» describes something that behaves differently from the rest and causes trouble, as in «a rogue wave» or «a rogue elephant».
fact: «Go rogue» means to stop following orders.
exercise: The Chairman calls Toad «the incorrigible rogue and hardened ruffian». What does rogue mean here? | A kind helper at the court | A tall tree in the forest | *A dishonest person who cheats or tricks people | A small bird
exercise: Grandad smiles and says, «You little rogue!» What does he mean? | A giant wave in the sea | *A naughty but likeable child | A grumpy judge | A wicked thief who steals gold
exercise: «A rogue wave» hit the boat. What does rogue mean in this phrase? | *Behaving differently from the rest and causing danger | Friendly and calm | Made of wood | Sleepy and slow
test: «The rogue elephant ran away from the herd.» Rogue here is an adjective. What does it suggest? | Very small and quiet | *Behaving differently from the rest and causing trouble | Happy to be with the herd | Good at singing
test: Which phrase means to stop following orders? | Go to sleep | Go home | Go slowly | *Go rogue
exam: Jay says rogue always means a bad criminal. Is he right? | Yes, but only in stories | *No, it can also mean a naughty but likeable child, or describe something behaving differently and causing trouble | Yes, always, and nothing else | No, it only ever means a big wave
